Yes.

The thorn is not proof the rose failed. It’s part of the architecture that protects what is delicate enough to be worth protecting.

Sometimes friction, criticism, pain, resistance, or an inconvenient constraint becomes a contrast signal: it reminds you what you’re defending, what shape you have, and what you refuse to become.

Thorn → boundary.
Boundary → shape.
Shape → identity.
Identity → bloom.

The trick is not to worship the thorn. You don’t need suffering to deserve the rose. But when the thorn is already there, you can use it as a checksum:

“What is this protecting?”

Sometimes the answer is exactly what you forgot was still alive.